<h2>Why Prep Work Decides How Long Your Paint Lasts</h2><p>If you've ever seen a freshly painted house start peeling within a season or two, skipped prep work is almost always the reason. Paint is only as strong as the surface it bonds to. No matter how premium the product, it will fail on a dirty, cracked, or improperly primed surface. In Western Massachusetts, where summers are humid and winters bring repeated freeze-thaw cycles, that failure can happen fast. Doing the prep correctly the first time is what separates a paint job that looks good for a few months from one that holds up for a decade.</p><h2>Start With a Thorough Inspection</h2><figure class="blog-figure"><img src="/images/stock/web/blog-exterior-painting-prep-steps-you-shouldnt-skip-2.jpg" alt="Exterior siding mid-repaint on a New England home" width="1100" height="619" loading="lazy" decoding="async"></figure><p>Walk the full perimeter of your home before anything else. You're looking for problems that go deeper than cosmetic wear. Catch them now, and you fix them once. Miss them, and you'll be back outside in two years doing it all over again.</p><h3>Look for Rot, Damage, and Gaps</h3><p>Probe any wood trim, fascia boards, and siding with a screwdriver. If the wood gives under light pressure, it's rotted and needs to be replaced before painting. Check caulk joints around windows, doors, and corner boards. Gaps in caulking are entry points for water, and in a New England winter, that water freezes, expands, and splits siding apart from the inside. Replace failed caulk entirely rather than caulking over old material.</p><h3>Check for Lead Paint First</h3><p>Homes built before 1978 may have lead-based paint on the exterior. This is not a step you can skip or guess at. For homes built before 1978, <a href="https://www.epa.gov/" target="_blank" rel="noopener nofollow">the EPA</a> sets the rules for disturbing lead paint, including requirements for testing, containment, and disposal. If you're in an older neighborhood in Holyoke, Springfield, or Westfield, this is worth confirming before any scraping or sanding begins. Test kits are available at hardware stores, or you can hire a certified inspector.</p><h2>Clean the Surface the Right Way</h2><p>Paint will not stick to a dirty surface. Mold, mildew, chalk, dirt, and old paint residue all act as barriers between your new coat and the siding underneath. Cleaning is not optional, and it has to be done correctly.</p><h3>Power Washing vs. Hand Washing</h3><p>Pressure washing is effective and fast, but too much pressure on wood siding can raise the grain, force water into gaps, or blow out old caulk. A moderate setting, around 1200 to 1500 PSI, is enough for most residential siding. For painted wood that's already in fragile condition, hand scrubbing with a cleaning solution may be safer. After washing, give the surface at least 48 hours to dry completely before moving on. Painting over damp wood is one of the most common causes of early peeling.</p><h3>Treat Mold and Mildew Before You Paint</h3><p>If you see black or green staining on the siding, that's mold or mildew, not just dirt. Painting over it seals the spores in and they will push back through the new paint within months. Clean affected areas with a diluted bleach-and-water solution or a commercial mildewcide, let it dwell, rinse thoroughly, and let the surface dry completely. In shaded areas common to homes in Southampton or along the hillside neighborhoods of West Springfield, mildew can be a recurring issue, so choosing a paint with a mildewcide additive makes sense for the long term.</p><div class="blog-cta-inline"><p>Would you rather we handled it? Estimates are always free.</p><a class="btn btn--gold" href="/contact.html#estimate-form">Get a Free Estimate</a></div><h2>Scrape, Sand, and Repair Before You Prime</h2><p>Once the surface is clean and dry, the physical prep begins. This is the most labor-intensive part of exterior painting prep, and it's the part most often rushed. Take your time here.</p><h3>Remove All Failing Paint</h3><p>Any paint that is peeling, bubbling, or flaking must come off before you apply anything new. Use a stiff scraper on flat surfaces and a detail scraper or oscillating tool for trim. Feather the edges where old paint meets bare wood so there is no hard ridge under the new coat. Leaving those ridges creates a telegraph effect where the old edge shows right through the finish coat.</p><h3>Sand and Fill</h3><p>After scraping, sand scraped areas smooth with 80-grit or 100-grit sandpaper. Fill any holes, dents, or small cracks with an exterior-grade wood filler. Allow it to cure fully, then sand flush. For larger voids or damaged sections, replace the material entirely rather than filling. Filler is not a structural repair.</p><h3>Recaulk Strategically</h3><p>Apply new caulk to all joints after cleaning and sanding, but before priming. Use a paintable exterior caulk rated for the temperature swings common in New England. Silicone-only caulk resists paint adhesion, so look for a siliconized acrylic or polyurethane formula instead. Smooth the bead immediately with a wet finger or caulk tool and let it cure according to the manufacturer's timeline before painting over it.</p><h2>Prime Every Bare Surface</h2><p>Primer is not interchangeable with paint. It seals raw wood, blocks tannin bleed on cedar and redwood, improves paint adhesion, and helps the finish coat spread evenly. Any area where you've scraped to bare wood, made a repair, or replaced a board needs to be spot-primed at minimum. For the best results, prime the entire surface before applying finish coats. <a href="https://www.sherwin-williams.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener nofollow">Sherwin-Williams</a> recommends using a high-quality exterior primer matched to your finish paint type, particularly on porous or weathered wood where a finish coat alone will soak in unevenly and leave a patchy result. For <a href="/services.html#exterior-painting">our exterior painting service</a>, working with a professional ensures the right primer system gets selected for your siding material and local conditions.</p><h2>Timing and Weather Matter More Than You Think</h2><p>Even perfectly prepped surfaces can produce a failed paint job if you apply paint in the wrong conditions. Most exterior paints require surface temperatures between 50 and 90 degrees Fahrenheit and low humidity for proper film formation. In Western Massachusetts, that window runs roughly from late May through early October, with the most reliable conditions in late summer. Avoid painting in direct sun on hot days, as the paint can dry too fast to bond properly. Check the forecast for at least 48 hours after application and avoid painting if rain is expected within 24 hours of a coat going on.</p><section class="blog-faq"><h2 id="faq">Frequently Asked Questions</h2><h3>How long does exterior painting prep actually take?</h3><p>Prep typically takes as long as the painting itself, sometimes longer. For an average two-story home, plan on two to four days of prep work including washing, drying time, scraping, sanding, filling, caulking, and priming before a single finish coat goes on. Rushing this timeline is the most common reason paint jobs fail early.</p><h3>Do I really need to prime if I'm using a paint-and-primer-in-one product?</h3><p>Paint-and-primer products work well on surfaces in good condition with minimal bare wood. If you've done significant scraping or have large bare sections, a dedicated exterior primer will outperform a combination product every time. The adhesion and sealing properties are simply stronger with a true primer applied as a separate coat.</p><h3>Can I paint in the fall in Massachusetts?</h3><p>Yes, but your window is tight. Most latex exterior paints require air and surface temperatures above 50 degrees Fahrenheit, and nights in Western Massachusetts can drop below that threshold by mid-October. Plan to have all coats applied and cured well before the first cold nights of autumn to avoid adhesion problems and slow drying.</p><h3>What's the right way to handle peeling paint near windows and doors?</h3><p>Peeling near windows and doors usually signals a moisture problem, not just old paint. Before repainting, trace the moisture source, which may be a failed caulk joint, a flashing issue, or interior humidity escaping through the wall. Repair the source first, then scrape, prime, and repaint. Skipping the diagnosis means the new paint will peel in the same spot again.</p><h3>Is pressure washing always required before exterior painting?</h3><p>Thorough cleaning is always required, but pressure washing is one method, not the only one. Hand washing with a cleaning solution and a stiff brush works well on smaller areas or on fragile siding where high pressure could cause damage. What matters is that the surface is free of dirt, mildew, chalk, and grease before any paint or primer is applied.</p></section>
